# Line endings and binary handling.
#
# Without this file, what lands in a commit depends on each contributor's local
# core.autocrlf setting, which is how a repository ends up with the same file
# committed twice in two spellings and a diff that touches every line.

# Store every text file with LF, and check it out with LF too. The `eol=lf` half is
# deliberate rather than the usual `native`: this project's tests run on Linux and
# macOS in CI, the working tree is already LF, and keeping checkout, index and CI
# byte-identical removes the whole class of "works on my machine" line-ending bug.
# Every editor on Windows has handled LF for years.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts would break outright with CRLF - bash reads the carriage return as
# part of the interpreter path - so this one is not merely a preference.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalise, never diff as text. The .xlsx files matter most here -
# they are zip containers, and rewriting bytes inside one corrupts the workbook.
*.ico  binary
*.png  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.xlsx binary

# Generated and resolved by `uv lock`, not written by hand. Marking it collapses the
# file in pull-request diffs, where it is noise, without excluding it from review.
uv.lock linguist-generated=true
